The Non-Poverty Action Plan of the UN World Food Programme (WFP) is a global blueprint for action on hunger and malnutrition.
A major report published in 2016 by WFP highlights the current state of food production, distribution, consumption and consumption patterns across nations. The report also examines how to improve access to essential commodities, including nutritious foods, that can help tackle food insecurity globally and ensure more people have access to healthy diets.
The WFP’s latest report was released on Wednesday at an event held at the United Nations Headquarters in New York City. It outlines five specific priorities to address:
- To increase access to nutritious food through targeted interventions such as micro-credit programs and community gardening initiatives;
- To accelerate progress towards gender equality within the agricultural sector and beyond through the adoption of new technologies such as crop breeding;
- To develop integrated approaches to poverty reduction, including promoting climate resilient agriculture practices;
- To strengthen national capacity for nutrition assessment and monitoring through investments in food-based research and innovative ways to deliver the WHO Global Nutrition Security Index; and
- To support countries with developing capacities to implement strategies to enhance nutrition security, particularly among vulnerable groups such as children.
